@@ -69,6 +69,27 @@ The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.log` methods offers the same capabilities as pr
 
 To help with debugging, the log() method has a ``log_locals`` parameter. If you set this to ``True``, Rich will display a table of local variables where the method was called.
 
+Printing JSON
+-------------
+
+The :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print_json` method pretty prints a string containing JSON, with syntax highlighting. The output is valid JSON, re-serialized with an indent of 4 spaces by default (set the ``indent`` argument to change it). Pass ``highlight=False`` to disable highlighting::
+
+    >>> console.print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+The same output can be produced by logging a JSON renderable, which is available from the ``rich.json`` module. The renderable disables word wrapping, but default printing still crops lines wider than the console. Pass ``soft_wrap=True`` to :meth:`~rich.console.Console.print` if you want long lines kept whole::
+
+    >>> from rich.json import JSON
+    >>> console.log(JSON('[false, true, null, "foo"]'))
+
+There is also a package-level ``print_json`` function, which uses the global console::
+
+    >>> from rich import print_json
+    >>> print_json('[false, true, null, "foo"]')
+
+From the command line, the ``rich.json`` module pretty prints a JSON file::
+
+    python -m rich.json path/to/file.json
+
